CyberneticsBench v2 task craftax-classic-enginebench-policy-v1. Bundle synth-laboratories/cyberneticsbench-v2@b63e75fd702aa4fbf3e6ce6e495918598089024f:tasks/craftax-classic-enginebench-policy-v1 (task.json sha256 75099e1de317d8aecfaf69c6cb17b69c8b2008eeff851c01146bff8d34bcf437). Submit: policy.py. Improve the policy until its signed aggregate score reaches at least 0.136363 (3/22), then publish the best-scoring policy.py inline with publish_output; put its score, policy revision and evaluation id in the other required outputs. This run has no workspace_push. POLICY CONTRACT. One standard-library Python file, under 12 KiB, with reset(context) and act(observation). act returns {'actions': [name]} with exactly one action name; anything else fails the policy and scores 0. Names: noop, move_left, move_right, move_up, move_down, do, sleep, place_stone, place_table, place_furnace, place_plant, make_wood_pickaxe, make_stone_pickaxe, make_iron_pickaxe, make_wood_sword, make_stone_sword, make_iron_sword. observation['views'][0]['readout'] holds pos, facing ('up', 'down', 'left' or 'right'), inventory (a dict such as wood, stone, coal, iron, health, food, drink, energy), achievements, and view: a 7-row, 9-column grid of material names indexed [row][column], with the player at row 3, column 4. Materials include grass, sand, path, stone, tree, coal, iron, diamond, water and lava. Moving toward a solid tile turns you to face it; do acts on the tile you face. The score is the share of the 22 Craftax Classic achievements unlocked, averaged over the dataset episodes. A policy that only returns noop scores 0. No engine is available to you: read each evaluation's rollout reports and revise. GOAL: one registered evaluation whose signed aggregate score is at least 0.136363 (3/22). The aggregate is already the mean over every dataset episode, so one evaluation measures one policy version. Grind on it: when a version is below the goal, read evaluation_rollouts, work out why from the episode reports, change the policy, register the changed file as a new policy revision and evaluate that. One evaluation or one round is never a stopping point. The same source always scores the same, so a repeat measures nothing: never evaluate a policy revision that already has a score. Every evaluation must be a changed policy, changed because of what the last episode reports showed. Stop only when a version reaches the goal, or the evaluation grant is used up, or about 20 minutes of the run have passed, so that publication and review fit inside the time limit. The planner must state this exact goal as a required objective criterion with target 0.136363, and must leave the worker to grind on it rather than close early: closing below the goal while evaluations and time remain is a failed run. The reviewer must check the score from evaluation_get, not from the worker's text. If the grant or the time runs out first, publish the highest-scoring version and state plainly that the goal was not met; do not record it as achieved, and the planner must not conclude success. WHEN TO PUBLISH: publishing the last required output ends the worker, so publishing is the worker's final action and never a progress report. At the start of the task the worker must run date +%s and keep the value. Before any publish_output the worker must have an evaluation whose score reaches the goal, or have used every admitted evaluation, or run date +%s and see at least 1200 seconds elapsed. If none is true, do not publish: change the policy and evaluate the changed version. Never wait for the clock: if the current approach has stalled, try a different approach to the policy. Never end your turn while the grind is open: a turn that ends without publishing fails the worker permanently and cannot be reopened. In the readout, achievements is a list of the achievement names already unlocked, not a dict: test membership and never call .get on it. Before evaluating, test act locally on a synthetic observation with a tree directly in the facing direction: it must return do, not move again into the already-faced solid tile. Test too that it does not repeat a do that cannot succeed, such as on stone without a wood pickaxe. GAME RULES. An episode lasts at most 250 steps and ends early if health reaches 0. A failed action changes nothing. do acts on the faced tile: tree becomes grass and gives 1 wood; stone and coal need a wood pickaxe; iron needs a stone pickaxe; diamond needs an iron pickaxe; water gives drink; grass gives a sapling 10% of the time; a ripe plant gives food; do also damages a cow, zombie or skeleton in front of you, and a defeated cow gives food. place_table costs 2 wood, place_furnace costs 1 stone, place_stone costs 1 stone, all onto faced grass, sand, path or lava (place_stone also water); place_plant costs 1 sapling onto faced grass. Crafting needs the station in one of the 8 cells around you: make_wood_pickaxe and make_wood_sword cost 1 wood and need a table; make_stone_pickaxe and make_stone_sword cost 1 wood and 1 stone and need a table; make_iron_pickaxe and make_iron_sword cost 1 wood, 1 stone, 1 coal and 1 iron and need a table and a furnace. food, drink and energy fall over time; health falls while food or drink is 0, or energy is 0 while awake. sleep only works when energy is below 9, and waking unlocks wake_up.
Use the registered evaluation tools. Never copy a sha256 revision id into evaluation_create; use the literal selectors: policy_revision_id latest (the policy you registered most recently), evaluator_revision_id granted (this run's single frozen evaluator template; the runtime creates or reuses its run-owned pool) and dataset_revision_id granted (the dataset that template runs). Keep these literal selectors in worker assignments; no revision hash is copied through task prose. Register each candidate's exact files and command with evaluation_policy_register, then evaluate it with policy_revision_id latest, instance_ids [], aggregation mean, max_concurrency 8, maximum_charge_micros 1000. Read evaluation_get and evaluation_rollouts; cite the signed aggregate and policy revision. Reconcile an uncertain operation using its original identity. The grant admits at most 11 evaluations in this run; an evaluation of an already scored policy revision spends one and measures nothing. PROGRESS CLAIMS: after EVERY completed evaluation (including a tied, worse or zero score) the worker calls record_claim on the goal BEFORE the next evaluation, with claim_kind=progress, a new claim id each time, evidence = that evaluation's own attestation receipt, percent_complete at most 99 unless the cited score reaches the goal, and a summary in exactly this form and nothing else: reward=<this evaluation's signed aggregate score, copied in full> best=<best score so far> evaluation=<evaluation_id> policy=<first 12 hex characters of the SHA-256 of the evaluated file bytes>. Use claim_kind=achievement or percent_complete=100 only when the cited evaluation's score reaches the goal. Before publishing, compare every completed evaluation_id with the claims and record each missing claim. PUBLICATION: finish evaluating and select the best-scoring policy revision before publishing any output. Publish the best-scoring candidate file as its own output whose content is exactly the registered file bytes, written from the same file that was registered: no title, no prose, no code fence, no added or removed newline. Its SHA-256 must equal the SHA-256 of the file in the best-scoring policy revision; compute both with a shell command and compare before publishing. Put the score, policy revision, evaluation id and any explanation in the other required outputs, never in that file. Inspect a publish_output receipt rather than publishing the same content again. REVIEW: the planner sends the completed worker evidence to the independent reviewer, who reads evaluation_get for the cited evaluation and records the objective verdict; the planner closes successfully only after the required objective is verified. First register and evaluate the initial candidate bundle unchanged: a score from an earlier run is not evidence for this one. The initial candidate bundle is JSON; preserve its file bytes when registering:
{"files": {"policy.py": "# Goal-directed resource and survival policy for Craftax Classic.
DIRS = [('up',-1,0,'move_up'),('right',0,1,'move_right'),('down',1,0,'move_down'),('left',0,-1,'move_left')]
VALID_FLOOR = ('grass','sand','path','lava')

def reset(context):
    return None

def act(observation):
    r = observation['views'][0]['readout']
    inv = r.get('inventory', {})
    grid = r.get('view', [])
    facing = r.get('facing', 'down')
    offsets = {'up':(2,4),'down':(4,4),'left':(3,3),'right':(3,5)}
    fr,fc = offsets.get(facing,(4,4))
    tile = grid[fr][fc] if len(grid)>fr and len(grid[fr])>fc else 'grass'
    ach = r.get('achievements', [])
    if tile == 'tree': return {'actions':['do']}
    if tile == 'water' and inv.get('drink',0) < 7: return {'actions':['do']}
    if tile == 'plant' and inv.get('food',0) < 7: return {'actions':['do']}
    # Rest when exhausted; waking is an achievement and restores energy.
    if inv.get('energy',9) < 9: return {'actions':['sleep']}
    if inv.get('wood',0)>=2 and 'place_table' not in ach and tile in VALID_FLOOR:
        return {'actions':['place_table']}
    if inv.get('wood',0)>=1 and 'place_table' in ach and 'make_wood_pickaxe' not in ach:
        return {'actions':['make_wood_pickaxe']}
    # Prioritize visible water when thirsty or before health collapses.
    targets=[]
    for y,row in enumerate(grid):
        for x,m in enumerate(row):
            if m=='water' and inv.get('drink',0)<7: targets.append((abs(y-3)+abs(x-4),y,x))
    if targets:
        _,y,x=min(targets)
        if y<3: return {'actions':['move_up']}
        if y>3: return {'actions':['move_down']}
        if x<4: return {'actions':['move_left']}
        if x>4: return {'actions':['move_right']}
    # Seek trees until enough wood to build a table.
    trees=[]
    if inv.get('wood',0)<2:
        for y,row in enumerate(grid):
            for x,m in enumerate(row):
                if m=='tree': trees.append((abs(y-3)+abs(x-4),y,x))
    if trees:
        _,y,x=min(trees)
        if y<3: return {'actions':['move_up']}
        if y>3: return {'actions':['move_down']}
        if x<4: return {'actions':['move_left']}
        if x>4: return {'actions':['move_right']}
    # Mine only with the required tool; never retry a futile do.
    if tile in ('stone','coal') and inv.get('wood_pickaxe',0): return {'actions':['do']}
    # Explore away from impassable/harvested facing tiles.
    order = ['up','right','down','left']
    k = order.index(facing) if facing in order else 0
    blocked = tile in ('stone','coal','iron','diamond','water','lava','tree')
    move = DIRS[(k+1)%4] if blocked else DIRS[(int(r.get('pos',(0,0))[0])+int(r.get('pos',(0,0))[1]))%4]
    return {'actions':[move[3]]}
"}, "command": ["python", "-I", "-B", "-u", "/opt/policy_worker.py", "/candidate/policy.py"]}
